        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:e4910c9e-60de-4571-afa0-7c8313cb30b9",
        "expected": "Most of my technical work has been in computational health, and the thing that keeps pulling me back is that healthcare systems fail in ways that only show up when real people use them. At Intelehealth I worked on AI and data systems for rural telemedicine, including a resource-availability scoring pipeline across 32 villages and an evaluation framework comparing LLM-generated clinical outputs against a 4,169-case standard. That work convinced me that generating an answer is the cheap part and deciding whether it should be trusted is the hard part. I also co-founded Open Health Intelligence, where I built ClarityAtlas end to end in Swift and SwiftUI with a passive sleep-estimation system using HealthKit and Core Motion signals, and shipped it into a live Hopkins pilot with 30+ users. Recruiting those users taught me more about product than the modeling did. Grow Therapy is interesting to me because 15 million sessions is an operating system for care delivery, not a demo, so the engineering problems are matching, scheduling, reliability, and trust under real constraints. I want to own a real piece of that kind of system rather than isolated tickets.",
        "observed": "Most of my technical work has been in computational health, and the thing that keeps pulling me back is that healthcare systems fail in ways that only show up when real people use them. At Intelehealth I worked on AI and data systems for rural telemedicine, including a resource-availability scoring pipeline across 32 villages and an evaluation framework comparing LLM-generated clinical outputs against a 4,169-case standard. That work convinced me that generating an answer is the cheap part and deciding whether it should be trusted is the hard part. I also co-founded Open Health Intelligence, where I built ClarityAtlas end to end in Swift and SwiftUI with a passive sleep-estimation system using HealthKit and Core Motion signals, and shipped it into a live Hopkins pilot with 30+ users. Recruiting those users taught me more about product than the modeling did. Grow Therapy is interesting to me because 15 million sessions is an operating system for care delivery, not a demo, so the engineering problems are matching, scheduling, reliability, and trust under real constraints. I want to own a real piece of that kind of system rather than isolated tickets.",
